issue.body:
When a Tom Select opens an input group, the visible wrapper gets square start corners and is pulled left by the border width.

Tom Select adds `.ts-hidden-accessible` to the original `<select>` and inserts the wrapper after it, so the hidden select stays the group's first child and the wrapper matches `.input-group > :not(:first-child)`.

Proposed fix:

```css
.input-group > .ts-hidden-accessible:first-child + .ts-wrapper {
  margin-inline-start: 0 !important;
  border-start-start-radius: var(--tblr-border-radius) !important;
  border-end-start-radius: var(--tblr-border-radius) !important;
}
```

`!important` is needed because the input-group rule has a much higher specificity.

Reported in user feedback on 1.7.

The Tom Select focus ring is drawn on `.ts-control`, which sits 1px inside the wrapper's border, and is rounded as much as the wrapper (`border-radius: inherit !important`, #3312). Its corners curve wider than the wrapper's inner curve, so the wrapper border shows outside the ring at each corner (visible at 10× zoom).

Proposed fix:

```css
.ts-wrapper:is(.form-control, .form-select):not(.input-group > *) .ts-control {
  border-radius: calc(var(--tblr-control-border-radius) - var(--tblr-control-border-width)) !important;
}
```

An input group is left alone because its wrapper corners differ. Moving the ring onto the wrapper, as native inputs have it, would be cleaner.

Confirmed in the code, not yet checked visually. Reported in user feedback on 1.7.
